# Northwind commerce

Northwind is an opt-in NetScript showcase. It uses the same Utility pages,
typed services and background plugins as an ordinary generated application.
The sample contains 40 orders, five customers, eight products and 80 line items.
Money is stored in integer minor units; totals come from the saved line items.

## Start the showcase

Install Deno 2.9.7, the pinned Aspire CLI and .NET SDK. Docker is needed for
PostgreSQL; SQLite can use the supported local Garnet fallback. Private chat
currently requires a POSIX host and the same OS user for setup and runtime. Run:

```sh
netscript init northwind --from showcase --ci --yes
cd northwind
deno task showcase:setup
deno task aspire:start
```

The setup task installs streams, workers, sagas, triggers, AI and authentication using the supported
plugin commands, copies the app-owned commerce resources, generates registries,
and initializes, generates and seeds the database. No manual linking or source
edits are required. SQLite is the default; `--db postgres` selects PostgreSQL.
The first PostgreSQL database lifecycle is provisioned through Aspire by the CLI.
Existing related orders are preserved on reseed.

Open the web app URL shown by Aspire. Overview shows saved orders, revenue,
status charts and activity. Orders supports search, status filters, sorting and
CSV export. Customers and Products read the same live database through oRPC.

## Follow an order

1. Open Orders, then an order that is pending, awaiting payment or paid.
2. Select **Fulfil order**. Refresh the order to see durable worker progress.
3. The order saga records **Order**, **Payment** and **Shipping** steps. Its
   payment is a demonstration transition, with no external charge.
4. Return to Overview. The stream activity includes the committed status changes.
5. The order detail also provides **Send delivery webhook**, which uses the
   registered webhook trigger to enqueue the same fulfilment job.

The webhook is deliberately public sample ingress for local development. Replace
the sample `memory` verifier with your verifier before exposing it outside your demo.
Sign in uses the normal authentication surface. External credentials are required
to complete a real provider login; no demo identity is supplied.

## Connect the assistant

Open **Assistant** (`/ai`). Without a supported model credential it shows the
provider connection state and makes no model call. The setup task already
installs its AI components, native server boundary, private streams host and
per-user conversation storage. No manual source linking or shared stream key is
needed. It stores the workspace's stream credential only in `~/.secrets` (600),
with non-secret metadata in `.netscript/showcase-streams.json`.

Use the default `anthropic:*` model in `ai/models.ts` with `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,
or select `openai-compatible:<model>` with `OPENAI_API_KEY` and optionally
`OPENAI_BASE_URL`. Keep credentials in your server environment or private secret
manager, then restart the app. Model names are owned by the installed AI plugin.

Private conversations require the default **kv-oauth** authentication backend.
Provide a stable `NETSCRIPT_AUTH_KV_OAUTH_KEY` (base64url-encoded 32 random bytes),
real OAuth/OIDC client credentials and endpoints, and register the web app's
`/auth/callback` URL with your provider. Follow the installed auth plugin README
for its provider settings and secure-cookie transport requirements. Use HTTPS
for deployment; local HTTP development requires `localhost` and the auth service's
explicit local transport opt-in. Configure the environment before starting
Aspire. An installed but unconfigured identity provider is not a working login.
The alternative better-auth backend currently validates existing sessions; it
does not provide this generated interactive sign-in path.

After signing in, create a conversation, ask which orders await payment, refresh
and reopen it. The agent uses the read-only `northwind_list_orders` and
`northwind_get_order` MCP tools against the actual orders service. The same tools
are available at the authenticated, same-origin POST endpoint
`/api/showcase/mcp`. Transcript reads, sends and MCP calls verify the existing
browser session; the browser never chooses a subject or receives the stream
credential. Keep `.netscript/showcase-chat.kv`, `.data/streams`, stream metadata
and the private credential across restarts. This local ownership store supports
one app host; use a shared ownership store for a multi-host deployment.

## Extend the application

Start here. `contracts/commerce.contract.ts` owns customer/product/line schemas;
`services/orders/src/commerce.ts` implements live reads. The standard orders
contract and repository remain under `contracts/versions/v1/` and
`services/orders/src/`. `database/{{dbEngine}}/scripts/seed-commerce.ts` owns the
catalogue; the ordinary sample seed owns the dated order backbone.

`workers/jobs/fulfil-order.ts`, `sagas/order-fulfilment/order-saga.ts` and
`triggers/order-webhook.ts` are the background extension points. The shared
Utility views remain under `apps/{{appName}}/routes/(_components)/`; the additive
order workflow view is `order-workflow.tsx`. `ai/showcase/` owns the assistant contract, tools and server composition;
`showcase/streams-host.ts` is the app-owned private stream boundary.
All plugin runtime code belongs to
the installed packages. `showcase/resources/` retains the original recipe sources.
